import QOU.MathlibExt.TowerSpanReduction

/-!
# `SandwichReduces` discharged from the braid relations

Bean `qou-301d`. This file removes the last hypothesis in the chain

    braid relations  ⟹  SandwichReduces  ⟹  TowerSpan  ⟹  DisjointBlockMultiplicative

by **proving** `TowerSpanReduction.SandwichReduces` for any `MarkovTrace` whose
generators satisfy the three defining relations of an Iwahori-Hecke tower.

`TowerSpanReduction` reduced the disjoint-block trace law to one local rewriting
rule — *a doubled generator with base material between it collapses back down*:

    SandwichReduces B m  :  ∀ c ∈ B,  g m * c * g m  ∈  B + span (sandwich B m)

and recorded honestly that the rule was **not proved for any tower with a growing
filtration**. It is proved here.

## The argument, and why it is not circular

The obvious attack — induct on words in `B` — does not close. `B` is generated by
`g n, …, g M`, and the case that resists is an element carrying **two or more**
occurrences of the adjacent generator `g M`: `g_{M+1} · (b₀ g_M b₁ g_M b₂) ·
g_{M+1}` has no braid move available until the middle `b₁` is itself analysed.
Working this by hand, one is pushed toward the `T_w` basis of the Hecke algebra
and the double-coset decomposition `W_{m+1} = W_m ⊔ W_m s_m W_m`, i.e. toward a
structure theorem Mathlib does not have.

The induction that does close runs **over the tower**, not over words, and the
key observation is that the work has already been done one level down:

* `TowerSpanReduction.towerSpan_of_sandwichReduces` shows `SandwichReduces` at
  level `M+1` gives the filtration step `shiftedUpTo n (M+1) ≤ P (shiftedUpTo n M) (M+1)`.
* That step says every element of the *next* stage is already of the form
  `u + Σ vᵢ · g_{M+1} · wᵢ` with `u, vᵢ, wᵢ` in the *previous* stage — i.e. it
  carries **at most one** occurrence of `g_{M+1}` by construction.
* And an element of that shape does reduce one level up, in two cases only:
  - `sandwich_far` — the `u` part commutes past `g_{M+2}` (far commutation) and
    the doubled generator collapses by the quadratic relation;
  - `sandwich_braid` — the sandwich part has its single `g_{M+1}` turned into a
    sandwich over `g_{M+2}` by one application of the braid relation.

So the multi-occurrence case never arises: it is *dissolved by the inductive
hypothesis* rather than analysed. This is the standard proof of
`H_{m+1} = H_m + H_m T_m H_m`, and the reason it needs no basis theorem is that
`TowerSpan` is a **spanning** statement (`≤`). Linear independence of the `T_w`
— the part that genuinely needs Coxeter length theory — is never used and is not
claimed here.

## What this establishes, precisely

`towerSpan_of_braidRelations` : a `MarkovTrace` with `BraidRelations` satisfies
`TowerSpan`, unconditionally.

`disjointBlock_of_braidRelations` : hence `DisjointBlockMultiplicative` —
`tr (a * b) = tr a * tr b` for `a` in level `n` and `b` in the shifted tail,
**including words with repeated top-level generators**, which was the exact
obstruction `DisjointBlockTraceLaw` identified and could not remove.

## 🛑 What this does NOT establish

* **It is not the bond product.** The disjoint-block law is one input to the
  bond-product reading of the atom word; the reading itself remains **measured,
  not derived** (bean `qou-qqm4`). Nothing here computes a binding coefficient.
* **`BraidRelations` is a hypothesis on the generators, not a theorem about
  them.** It is discharged for the presented tower `HeckeTowerWitness.HeckeTower`
  in `SandwichReducesHeckeInstance`, where the three relations hold *by
  construction* — that is a genuine `h ≠ 0` instance, but a presented algebra is
  the easy direction. Whether the *Ocneanu* trace on `H_∞(q)` satisfies
  `MarkovTrace` faithfully is Jones' theorem and remains open (bean `qou-g8i1`).
* **No claim of linear independence.** `TowerSpan` is `≤`, not `=`, and the
  proof below uses only spanning.

-- Ref: [jones1987] https://doi.org/10.2307/1971403
-- Ref: [manuscript] bean qou-301d.
-/

namespace QOU.MathlibExt.SandwichReducesFromBraid

open QOU.MathlibExt.OcneanuTrace QOU.MathlibExt.DisjointBlockTrace
open QOU.MathlibExt.TowerSpanReduction

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {A : Type*} [Ring A] [Algebra R A]
variable (T : MarkovTrace R A)

/-- **The three defining relations of an Iwahori-Hecke tower**, as a hypothesis
on a `MarkovTrace`'s generators. `h` is the quadratic scalar (`q - q⁻¹` in the
Hecke normalisation); `h = 0` is the symmetric-group specialisation.

This is a hypothesis class in the §3b-cond sense: it carries the *inputs* (three
equations between products of generators), and the theorems below **derive** the
filtration and trace statements from them. It does not carry any conclusion. -/
class BraidRelations (h : R) : Prop where
  /-- `σ_m² = h·σ_m + 1`. -/
  quadratic : ∀ m, T.g m * T.g m = h • T.g m + 1
  /-- `σ_{m+1} σ_m σ_{m+1} = σ_m σ_{m+1} σ_m`. -/
  braid : ∀ m, T.g (m + 1) * T.g m * T.g (m + 1) = T.g m * T.g (m + 1) * T.g m
  /-- `σ_m σ_j = σ_j σ_m` for `j + 1 < m`. -/
  farComm : ∀ m j, j + 1 < m → T.g m * T.g j = T.g j * T.g m

/-! ## The two local cases -/

/-- **Far case.** A base element that commutes with the new generator is pushed
straight through, and the doubled generator collapses by the quadratic relation:
`g c g = c g g = h·(c g) + c`. -/
theorem sandwich_far (B : Subalgebra R A) (m : ℕ) (h : R)
    (hquad : T.g m * T.g m = h • T.g m + 1)
    {c : A} (hc : c ∈ B) (hcomm : T.g m * c = c * T.g m) :
    T.g m * c * T.g m ∈ P T B m := by
  have e : T.g m * c * T.g m = h • (c * T.g m) + c := by
    rw [hcomm, mul_assoc, hquad, mul_add, mul_smul_comm, mul_one]
  rw [e]
  refine Submodule.add_mem _ (Submodule.smul_mem _ _ ?_) (base_le_P T B m hc)
  exact Submodule.mem_sup_right (Submodule.subset_span ⟨c, hc, 1, B.one_mem, by simp⟩)

/-- **Braid case.** A single occurrence of the adjacent generator, flanked by
material that commutes with the new one, becomes a sandwich outright:
`g_{M+1} (v g_M w) g_{M+1} = v (g_{M+1} g_M g_{M+1}) w = (v g_M) g_{M+1} (g_M w)`. -/
theorem sandwich_braid (B : Subalgebra R A) (M : ℕ)
    (hbraid : T.g (M + 1) * T.g M * T.g (M + 1) = T.g M * T.g (M + 1) * T.g M)
    (hgM : T.g M ∈ B)
    {v w : A} (hv : v ∈ B) (hw : w ∈ B)
    (hcv : T.g (M + 1) * v = v * T.g (M + 1))
    (hcw : T.g (M + 1) * w = w * T.g (M + 1)) :
    T.g (M + 1) * (v * T.g M * w) * T.g (M + 1) ∈ P T B (M + 1) := by
  have e : T.g (M + 1) * (v * T.g M * w) * T.g (M + 1)
      = (v * T.g M) * T.g (M + 1) * (T.g M * w) := by
    calc T.g (M + 1) * (v * T.g M * w) * T.g (M + 1)
        = v * (T.g (M + 1) * T.g M * T.g (M + 1)) * w := by
          rw [show T.g (M + 1) * (v * T.g M * w) * T.g (M + 1)
                = T.g (M + 1) * v * (T.g M * (w * T.g (M + 1))) by
                simp only [mul_assoc], hcv, ← hcw]
          simp only [mul_assoc]
      _ = v * (T.g M * T.g (M + 1) * T.g M) * w := by rw [hbraid]
      _ = (v * T.g M) * T.g (M + 1) * (T.g M * w) := by simp only [mul_assoc]
  rw [e]
  exact Submodule.mem_sup_right (Submodule.subset_span
    ⟨v * T.g M, B.mul_mem hv hgM, T.g M * w, B.mul_mem hgM hw, rfl⟩)

/-- **The two cases together.** Anything already decomposed as *previous stage +
single sandwiches over the adjacent generator* reduces one level up.

This is the whole mathematical content: an element of `P F M` carries at most one
occurrence of `g M`, and each of the two shapes has exactly one move available. -/
theorem sandwich_reduces_of_oneStep (B F : Subalgebra R A) (M : ℕ) (h : R)
    (hquad : T.g (M + 1) * T.g (M + 1) = h • T.g (M + 1) + 1)
    (hbraid : T.g (M + 1) * T.g M * T.g (M + 1) = T.g M * T.g (M + 1) * T.g M)
    (hFB : F ≤ B) (hgM : T.g M ∈ B)
    (hfar : ∀ c ∈ F, T.g (M + 1) * c = c * T.g (M + 1))
    {c : A} (hc : c ∈ P T F M) :
    T.g (M + 1) * c * T.g (M + 1) ∈ P T B (M + 1) := by
  rw [P, Submodule.mem_sup] at hc
  obtain ⟨u, hu, s, hs, rfl⟩ := hc
  have hu' : u ∈ F := by simpa using hu
  rw [mul_add, add_mul]
  refine Submodule.add_mem _ (sandwich_far T B (M + 1) h hquad (hFB hu') (hfar u hu')) ?_
  induction hs using Submodule.span_induction with
  | mem x hx =>
      obtain ⟨v, hv, w, hw, rfl⟩ := hx
      exact sandwich_braid T B M hbraid hgM (hFB hv) (hFB hw) (hfar v hv) (hfar w hw)
  | zero => simp
  | add x y _ _ ihx ihy => rw [mul_add, add_mul]; exact Submodule.add_mem _ ihx ihy
  | smul r x _ ihx => rw [mul_smul_comm, smul_mul_assoc]; exact Submodule.smul_mem _ _ ihx

/-! ## Getting the "one step down" hypothesis: the tower induction -/

/-- Adjoining the new generator (and its inverse) to `B` stays inside `P`, once
sandwiches reduce over `B`. This is the filtration step of
`towerSpan_of_sandwichReduces`, isolated so the induction below can use it at a
single index. -/
theorem adjoin_gen_le_P (B : Subalgebra R A) (m : ℕ) (h : R)
    (hquad : T.g m * T.g m = h • T.g m + 1)
    (hred : SandwichReduces T B m)
    {x : A} (hx : x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R ((B : Set A) ∪ {T.g m, T.ginv m})) :
    x ∈ P T B m := by
  have hone : (1 : A) ∈ P T B m := base_le_P T B m B.one_mem
  let S : Subalgebra R A :=
    Submodule.toSubalgebra (P T B m) hone (fun _ _ ha hb => P_mul_mem T B m hred ha hb)
  have hsub : Algebra.adjoin R ((B : Set A) ∪ {T.g m, T.ginv m}) ≤ S := by
    refine Algebra.adjoin_le ?_
    rintro y (hy | hy)
    · exact base_le_P T B m hy
    · rcases hy with rfl | rfl
      · exact gen_mem_P T B m
      · show T.ginv m ∈ P T B m
        rw [T.gen_inv_eq_of_quadratic m h hquad]
        exact Submodule.sub_mem _ (gen_mem_P T B m) (Submodule.smul_mem _ _ hone)
  exact hsub hx

/-- The filtration step lands in the adjoin of the previous stage with the new
generator. -/
theorem shiftedUpTo_le_adjoin (n M : ℕ) :
    shiftedUpTo T n (M + 1) ≤
      Algebra.adjoin R ((shiftedUpTo T n M : Set A) ∪ {T.g (M + 1), T.ginv (M + 1)}) := by
  refine Algebra.adjoin_le ?_
  rintro x ⟨j, hnj, hjM, rfl | rfl⟩
  · rcases Nat.lt_or_ge j (M + 1) with hlt | hge
    · exact Algebra.subset_adjoin
        (Or.inl (Algebra.subset_adjoin ⟨j, hnj, by omega, Or.inl rfl⟩))
    · obtain rfl : j = M + 1 := by omega
      exact Algebra.subset_adjoin (Or.inr (by simp))
  · rcases Nat.lt_or_ge j (M + 1) with hlt | hge
    · exact Algebra.subset_adjoin
        (Or.inl (Algebra.subset_adjoin ⟨j, hnj, by omega, Or.inr rfl⟩))
    · obtain rfl : j = M + 1 := by omega
      exact Algebra.subset_adjoin (Or.inr (by simp))

/-- The zero stage lands in the adjoin of `⊥` with the level-0 generator. -/
theorem shiftedUpTo_zero_le_adjoin (n : ℕ) :
    shiftedUpTo T n 0 ≤
      Algebra.adjoin R (((⊥ : Subalgebra R A) : Set A) ∪ {T.g 0, T.ginv 0}) := by
  refine Algebra.adjoin_le ?_
  rintro x ⟨j, hnj, hjM, rfl | rfl⟩
  · obtain rfl : j = 0 := by omega
    exact Algebra.subset_adjoin (Or.inr (by simp))
  · obtain rfl : j = 0 := by omega
    exact Algebra.subset_adjoin (Or.inr (by simp))

/-- A stage whose generator range is empty is `⊥`. -/
theorem shiftedUpTo_eq_bot (n M : ℕ) (hM : M < n) : shiftedUpTo T n M = ⊥ := by
  have hset : {x : A | ∃ m, n ≤ m ∧ m ≤ M ∧ (x = T.g m ∨ x = T.ginv m)} = ∅ := by
    ext x
    simp only [Set.mem_setOf_eq, Set.mem_empty_iff_false, iff_false]
    rintro ⟨m, hnm, hmM, _⟩
    omega
  rw [shiftedUpTo, hset, Algebra.adjoin_empty]

variable {T} {h : R}

/-- Far commutation extends to the inverse generator, via `ginv = g - h·1`. -/
theorem farComm_ginv [BraidRelations T h] (m j : ℕ) (hj : j + 1 < m) :
    T.g m * T.ginv j = T.ginv j * T.g m := by
  rw [T.gen_inv_eq_of_quadratic j h (BraidRelations.quadratic j), mul_sub, sub_mul,
    BraidRelations.farComm (T := T) (h := h) m j hj, mul_smul_comm, smul_mul_assoc,
    mul_one, one_mul]

/-- Far commutation lifts from the generators to the whole stage, because the
centralizer of `g k` is a subalgebra. -/
theorem far_shiftedUpTo [BraidRelations T h] (n M k : ℕ) (hk : M + 1 < k)
    {c : A} (hc : c ∈ shiftedUpTo T n M) : T.g k * c = c * T.g k := by
  have hle : shiftedUpTo T n M ≤ Subalgebra.centralizer R ({T.g k} : Set A) := by
    refine Algebra.adjoin_le ?_
    rintro x ⟨j, _, hjM, rfl | rfl⟩
    · exact fun y hy => by
        rcases hy with rfl; exact BraidRelations.farComm (T := T) (h := h) k j (by omega)
    · exact fun y hy => by
        rcases hy with rfl; exact farComm_ginv (h := h) k j (by omega)
  exact hle hc (T.g k) rfl

/-- Below the tower's own base every stage is `⊥`, where the far case settles it
outright. -/
theorem sandwichReduces_of_lt [BraidRelations T h] (n M : ℕ) (hM : M < n) :
    SandwichReduces T (shiftedUpTo T n M) (M + 1) := by
  intro c hc
  have hbot := shiftedUpTo_eq_bot T n M hM
  have hc' : c ∈ (⊥ : Subalgebra R A) := hbot ▸ hc
  obtain ⟨r, rfl⟩ := Algebra.mem_bot.1 hc'
  exact sandwich_far T _ (M + 1) h (BraidRelations.quadratic (M + 1)) hc
    (Algebra.commutes r (T.g (M + 1))).symm

/-- **`SandwichReduces` holds at every level**, by induction over the tower.

The inductive hypothesis is used *not* to analyse a harder word but to guarantee
that every element of the current stage is already in one-sandwich form, at which
point only `sandwich_far` and `sandwich_braid` are needed. -/
theorem sandwichReduces_shiftedUpTo [BraidRelations T h] (n : ℕ) :
    ∀ M : ℕ, SandwichReduces T (shiftedUpTo T n M) (M + 1) := by
  intro M
  induction M with
  | zero =>
      rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos n with rfl | hn
      · intro c hc
        have hbot : SandwichReduces T (⊥ : Subalgebra R A) 0 := by
          intro d hd
          obtain ⟨r, rfl⟩ := Algebra.mem_bot.1 hd
          exact sandwich_far T ⊥ 0 h (BraidRelations.quadratic 0) hd
            (Algebra.commutes r (T.g 0)).symm
        have hc' : c ∈ P T (⊥ : Subalgebra R A) 0 :=
          adjoin_gen_le_P T ⊥ 0 h (BraidRelations.quadratic 0) hbot
            (shiftedUpTo_zero_le_adjoin T 0 hc)
        refine sandwich_reduces_of_oneStep T (shiftedUpTo T 0 0) ⊥ 0 h
          (BraidRelations.quadratic 1) (BraidRelations.braid (T := T) (h := h) 0) bot_le ?_ ?_ hc'
        · exact Algebra.subset_adjoin ⟨0, le_rfl, le_rfl, Or.inl rfl⟩
        · intro d hd
          obtain ⟨r, rfl⟩ := Algebra.mem_bot.1 hd
          exact (Algebra.commutes r (T.g 1)).symm
      · exact sandwichReduces_of_lt (h := h) n 0 hn
  | succ M ih =>
      rcases Nat.lt_or_ge (M + 1) n with hlt | hge
      · exact sandwichReduces_of_lt (h := h) n (M + 1) hlt
      · intro c hc
        have hc' : c ∈ P T (shiftedUpTo T n M) (M + 1) :=
          adjoin_gen_le_P T _ (M + 1) h (BraidRelations.quadratic (M + 1)) ih
            (shiftedUpTo_le_adjoin T n M hc)
        refine sandwich_reduces_of_oneStep T (shiftedUpTo T n (M + 1)) (shiftedUpTo T n M)
          (M + 1) h (BraidRelations.quadratic (M + 2))
          (BraidRelations.braid (T := T) (h := h) (M + 1))
          (shiftedUpTo_mono T n (Nat.le_succ M)) ?_ ?_ hc'
        · exact Algebra.subset_adjoin ⟨M + 1, hge, le_rfl, Or.inl rfl⟩
        · exact fun d hd => far_shiftedUpTo (h := h) n M (M + 2) (by omega) hd

/-! ## The conclusions -/

/-- **`TowerSpan` holds for any Hecke tower.** The hypothesis
`TowerSpanReduction.towerSpan_of_sandwichReduces` left open is now discharged. -/
theorem towerSpan_of_braidRelations [BraidRelations T h] : TowerSpan T :=
  towerSpan_of_sandwichReduces T
    (fun m => ⟨h, BraidRelations.quadratic m⟩)
    (fun n M => sandwichReduces_shiftedUpTo (h := h) n M)

/-- **The disjoint-block trace law, unconditionally, for any Hecke tower.**

`tr (a * b) = tr a * tr b` whenever `a` sits at level `n` and `b` in the tail
generated from level `n` upward — **including** words with repeated top-level
generators, which is the case `DisjointBlockTraceLaw` could reach by no rotation
argument.

🛑 This is the trace law only. It is one input to the bond-product reading of the
atom word (`qou-qqm4`); that reading remains measured, not derived. -/
theorem disjointBlock_of_braidRelations [BraidRelations T h] :
    DisjointBlockMultiplicative T :=
  letI : TowerSpan T := towerSpan_of_braidRelations (h := h)
  disjointBlock_of_towerSpan T (fun m => ⟨h, BraidRelations.quadratic m⟩)

end QOU.MathlibExt.SandwichReducesFromBraid
